largest and fastest growing

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:30.750
social media platforms.

00:00:30.750 --> 00:00:33.900
But as the app and its algorithm
become more ubiquitous,

00:00:33.900 --> 00:00:37.200
we're learning in real time
how it's affecting our society

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:39.060
and even our brains.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:41.910
And some worry it's
doing so for the worst.

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:44.280
- Who created the air fryer?

00:00:44.280 --> 00:00:45.690
Really?

00:00:45.690 --> 00:00:47.850
Really, because I can't do it anymore.

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:51.120
- Are these concerns
legit or a tad overblown?

00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:53.550
Let's find out in this Wisecrack edition,

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:55.230
is TikTok evil?

00:00:55.230 --> 00:00:56.190
But before we dive in,

00:00:56.190 --> 00:00:59.340
I wanna tell you about
this video sponsor, Wren.

00:00:59.340 --> 00:01:00.630
When I look at the news lately,

00:01:00.630 --> 00:01:03.960
things are not great in a lot of ways.

00:01:03.960 --> 00:01:06.000
But among them is the ever looming threat

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.200
of climate disaster.

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.610
And it's easy to feel helpless,

00:01:08.610 --> 00:01:11.610
but Wren is an easy way
to help turn your concern

00:01:11.610 --> 00:01:13.500
for the climate into tangible action.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:16.952
First, Wren's easy quiz helps
you calculate the exact size

00:01:16.952 --> 00:01:18.450
of your carbon footprint.

00:01:18.450 --> 00:01:20.190
They'll ask you about
things like your diet,

00:01:20.190 --> 00:01:22.320
your travel habits, and
other lifestyle basics.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.380
From there, they suggest ways
for you to reduce your impact

00:01:25.380 --> 00:01:27.990
on the environment right off the bat.

00:01:27.990 --> 00:01:28.950
For instance,

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:30.856
I should really stop
driving short distances

00:01:30.856 --> 00:01:33.870
to pick up things like burritos
when I could just walk.

00:01:33.870 --> 00:01:36.780
But because nobody's carbon
footprint can be zero,

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:38.850
Wren also offsets your remaining footprint

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:40.650
with their targeted climate initiatives.

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:42.270
As someone who lives in California,

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:45.390
I was really impressed with
Wren's Biochar project,

00:01:45.390 --> 00:01:46.424
which removes dead trees

00:01:46.424 --> 00:01:49.003
from Fredwood forest and
high fire risk areas.

00:01:49.003 --> 00:01:50.704
They turn these trees into biochar,

00:01:50.704 --> 00:01:54.270
a stable form of carbon that
lasts for thousands of years.

00:01:54.270 --> 00:01:56.310
And while I'm extra pumped
about Wren's projects

00:01:56.310 --> 00:01:57.450
that are local to me,

00:01:57.450 --> 00:01:59.430
their work resonates around the world.

00:01:59.430 --> 00:02:01.260
There's their mineral weathering project

00:02:01.260 --> 00:02:02.580
in Scotland, for instance,

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.490
which turns atmospheric
carbon into solid rock,

00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:08.820
all using accelerated
natural geological processes,

00:02:08.820 --> 00:02:10.500
which is cool and all,

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:12.390
but this project also includes a brigade

00:02:12.390 --> 00:02:16.170
of wooly pigs who prepare
the soil for regrowth,

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:18.180
and they are just so adorable.

00:02:18.180 --> 00:02:20.190
Once you sign up to make
a monthly contribution

00:02:20.190 --> 00:02:22.500
that will be used to offset
your carbon footprint,

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:23.790
you'll receive monthly updates

00:02:23.790 --> 00:02:25.050
from the projects you support.

00:02:25.050 --> 00:02:28.380
So you will actually see the
impact that you are making.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:30.300
Wren has pledge to plant 10 extra trees

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:32.460
for the first 100 people who sign up

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.110
using our referral link.

00:02:34.110 --> 00:02:38.610
So go to wren.co/start/wisecrack

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:40.710
to join us in saving the rainforest

00:02:40.710 --> 00:02:42.060
and fighting the climate crisis.

00:02:42.060 --> 00:02:45.768
That's wren.co/start/wisecrack.

00:02:45.768 --> 00:02:47.820
And now, back to the show.

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:49.950
There's a lot of debate
about whether TikTok

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:52.860
is a societal good or a societal evil.

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:54.000
On the downside,

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:56.730
it's been shown to increase
anxiety, depression,

00:02:56.730 --> 00:02:58.080
and body image issues.

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:00.480
And though, I don't know, it
also allows a Chinese company

00:03:00.480 --> 00:03:02.308
to collect your personal data and possibly

00:03:02.308 --> 00:03:05.430
maybe collude with the
Chinese government for funds,

00:03:05.430 --> 00:03:07.440
censorship, or propaganda reasons,

00:03:07.440 --> 00:03:10.080
then again, Facebook
misinformation played a key role

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:12.900
in influencing elections,
but Hey, who's keeping score?

00:03:12.900 --> 00:03:15.000
But TikTok, isn't all bad.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:16.290
It's great for making friends

00:03:16.290 --> 00:03:18.780
and expanding your community
of people with a niche interest

00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:21.270
like a Midwest mom cooking hacks.

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:23.670
Plus, it's an accessible creative outlet

00:03:23.670 --> 00:03:25.200
that's super entertaining.

00:03:25.200 --> 00:03:26.800
- [Woman] Look at the butterfly.

00:03:28.256 --> 00:03:29.251
- That was stupid.

00:03:29.251 --> 00:03:31.488
- [Micheal] I mean, where
else could you see this?

00:03:31.488 --> 00:03:34.238
(dramatic music)

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:38.910
Or this?

00:03:38.910 --> 00:03:41.160
- I added these tassels to it,

00:03:41.160 --> 00:03:43.860
and our lives have never been the same.

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:44.693
- Or this?

00:03:44.693 --> 00:03:47.940
(bouncy music)

00:03:47.940 --> 00:03:51.210
I could go on for literal
hours, but I'll stop now.

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:53.645
And it's this level of
addiction that sets TikTok apart

00:03:53.645 --> 00:03:55.860
from other social media platforms.

00:03:55.860 --> 00:03:58.590
TikTok has a top-of-the-line algorithm

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:01.020
that consistently gives you what you want.

00:04:01.020 --> 00:04:03.360
This never-ending quest for dopamine

00:04:03.360 --> 00:04:06.531
is what some researchers now
refer to as TikTok brain.

00:04:06.531 --> 00:04:08.280
(dramatic music)

00:04:08.280 --> 00:04:10.140
And parents and experts are worried

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:12.870
about the impact it has on
kids' shrinking attention spans.

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:14.820
TikTok hasn't been around long enough

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:17.820
for researchers to discern
whether it permanently alters

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:20.627
or damages kids' brains, but
we couldn't help but notice

00:04:20.627 --> 00:04:23.130
these concerns are reminiscent of debates

00:04:23.130 --> 00:04:26.100
over whether violent video
games cause increased aggression

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:28.687
or whether heavy metal gives
you an unquenchable thirst

00:04:28.687 --> 00:04:29.760
for goat blood.

00:04:29.760 --> 00:04:31.456
So is it possible we're projecting

00:04:31.456 --> 00:04:35.490
our deeply embedded
societal fears onto TikTok?

00:04:35.490 --> 00:04:37.230
Odds are yes.

00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:40.380
Fear almost always rides on the
coattails of new technology.

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:42.067
For example, Plato and Socrates

00:04:42.067 --> 00:04:45.360
both believed writing would
destroy the oral tradition

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:46.650
and make everyone forgetful.

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:48.000
When trains were first invented,

00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:49.344
everyone thought centrifugal force

00:04:49.344 --> 00:04:51.120
would make women's uterus fly out.

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:53.100
They didn't do that.

00:04:53.100 --> 00:04:54.390
And in the late 19th century,

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:57.270
experts warned that riding
a bicycle could result

00:04:57.270 --> 00:04:59.863
in bicycle face, a
condition where your face

00:04:59.863 --> 00:05:03.870
would permanently look
blown back by a mighty wind.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:07.260
There was even a CNN
article published in 2005

00:05:07.260 --> 00:05:10.980
that said email hurt the
IQ more than marijuana.

00:05:10.980 --> 00:05:13.080
These hypotheses all beg the question.

00:05:13.080 --> 00:05:16.020
Why are we so freaked out by technology?

00:05:16.020 --> 00:05:19.890
And is TikTok the latest
centrifugal uterus ejecting force

00:05:19.890 --> 00:05:21.270
in human development?

00:05:21.270 --> 00:05:23.040
Prior to the 18th century,

00:05:23.040 --> 00:05:24.990
science and technology were separate

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:26.820
non-threatening entities.

00:05:26.820 --> 00:05:29.370
Science was mostly based in heady theories

00:05:29.370 --> 00:05:31.170
while technology was composed of skills

00:05:31.170 --> 00:05:33.030
passed down through the generations

00:05:33.030 --> 00:05:35.340
like blacksmithing or cobbling.

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:38.790
Then in a classic romcom
meet cute scenario,

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:41.430
18th century scientists,
Priestley and Lavoisier,

00:05:41.430 --> 00:05:43.020
introduced these fields to each other

00:05:43.020 --> 00:05:44.219
via chemical discoveries

00:05:44.219 --> 00:05:46.920
that led to large scale electricity.

00:05:46.920 --> 00:05:50.370
Just like that, science
had practical applications

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:53.130
and technology had a role
in scientific progress.

00:05:53.130 --> 00:05:54.750
They completed each other.

00:05:54.750 --> 00:05:56.550
By the beginning of the 19th century,

00:05:56.550 --> 00:05:59.550
everyone was hyped on
this new technology thing.

00:05:59.550 --> 00:06:03.180
Scientists and craftsman
intersected to create the engineer

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:05.820
and the world seemed
full of possibilities.

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:08.070
Flying machines, submarines,

00:06:08.070 --> 00:06:10.110
and the ability to talk to the dead

00:06:10.110 --> 00:06:12.630
were surely just around the corner, right?

00:06:12.630 --> 00:06:15.030
Technology transformed into a chiral

00:06:15.030 --> 00:06:17.760
for the human condition,
society, and the world,

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:20.520
which is exactly how I
used to feel about improv.

00:06:20.520 --> 00:06:22.710
Yes, and much like improv,

00:06:22.710 --> 00:06:25.560
the movement picked up
cult level enthusiasm.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:28.200
It became known as positivism.

00:06:28.200 --> 00:06:31.320
Auguste Comte, a French
philosopher and positivist,

00:06:31.320 --> 00:06:33.450
dreamed of a utopian society

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:35.640
where all the best
scientists were in power

00:06:35.640 --> 00:06:37.590
and that they unified everyone

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:39.870
through scientific and political progress.

00:06:39.870 --> 00:06:41.130
If that were true today,

00:06:41.130 --> 00:06:43.470
we'd be reciting the scientific method

00:06:43.470 --> 00:06:44.951
instead of the pledge of allegiance,

00:06:44.951 --> 00:06:48.300
which when I really think
about it, wouldn't be so bad.

00:06:48.300 --> 00:06:51.270
Unfortunately, before this
utopia could take shape,

00:06:51.270 --> 00:06:54.120
people's enthusiasm and
excitement for technology

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:55.980
took a turn for the worse.

00:06:55.980 --> 00:06:57.840
The start of the Industrial Revolution

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:00.630
kicked off a rise in
collective tech anxiety.

00:07:00.630 --> 00:07:02.250
The landscape of people's lives,

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:05.910
literally and figuratively,
seemed to transform overnight.

00:07:05.910 --> 00:07:07.260
The new bourgeois emerged,

00:07:07.260 --> 00:07:09.420
and as they profited off new technologies,

00:07:09.420 --> 00:07:12.210
they didn't promote peace,
fairness, and equality

00:07:12.210 --> 00:07:13.410
like people expected.

00:07:13.410 --> 00:07:16.320
Instead, they scooped up
their money without regard

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:18.450
for how it affected people around them.

00:07:18.450 --> 00:07:20.910
Meanwhile, workers were
afraid of losing their jobs

00:07:20.910 --> 00:07:23.910
to machines and didn't
have any social protections

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:24.870
if that did happen.

00:07:24.870 --> 00:07:26.310
People's anxiety over tech

00:07:26.310 --> 00:07:28.680
boiled down to three big questions.

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:31.010
One, could technology be destructive

00:07:31.010 --> 00:07:32.550
in the hands of the wrong people?

00:07:32.550 --> 00:07:35.820
Two, could this power
make humans into gods?

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:38.647
And three, could humans
be automated out of a job

00:07:38.647 --> 00:07:40.110
or purpose?

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:42.270
Then a couple of world wars came around,

00:07:42.270 --> 00:07:43.560
you may have heard of them,

00:07:43.560 --> 00:07:45.071
and answered those first two questions

00:07:45.071 --> 00:07:47.190
with a resounding yes.

00:07:47.190 --> 00:07:49.440
It became crystal clear that humans could

00:07:49.440 --> 00:07:51.912
and would use science and technology

00:07:51.912 --> 00:07:54.330
to kill millions of people.

00:07:54.330 --> 00:07:56.940
While the rise of the
internet in Silicon Valley

00:07:56.940 --> 00:07:59.550
revived a vision of technological utopia,

00:07:59.550 --> 00:08:03.000
anxiety surrounding tech
would never vanish completely.

00:08:03.000 --> 00:08:05.280
Something about lightning fast innovation

00:08:05.280 --> 00:08:07.830
continues to freak a lot of us out.

00:08:07.830 --> 00:08:10.140
Philosophers included, Martin Heidegger,

00:08:10.140 --> 00:08:13.650
a German philosopher who we
must note was in the Nazi Party,

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:16.380
was one of the first thinkers
to associate technology

00:08:16.380 --> 00:08:18.840
with the devastation of humanity's desire

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:20.220
for truth and meaning.

00:08:20.220 --> 00:08:22.920
Heidegger theorized that
technology pulls us away

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:25.200
from the true meaning of human existence,

00:08:25.200 --> 00:08:26.700
which he called dasein,

00:08:26.700 --> 00:08:29.580
which describes our way
of being in the world,

00:08:29.580 --> 00:08:32.760
always marked by the fact
that our being is oriented

00:08:32.760 --> 00:08:36.240
towards our inevitable
finitude, i.e. our death.

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:37.170
In other words,

00:08:37.170 --> 00:08:39.480
Heidegger thought that when
we stop looking for meaning

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:41.340
and things like art and poetry

00:08:41.340 --> 00:08:44.490
and instead rely on technology
to make sense of the world,

00:08:44.490 --> 00:08:47.490
things start to have a
primarily instrumental value.

00:08:47.490 --> 00:08:49.800
The next generation of German philosophers

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:51.660
doubled down on this idea.

00:08:51.660 --> 00:08:52.740
The Frankfurt School theorized

00:08:52.740 --> 00:08:55.560
that post-war capitalist societies

00:08:55.560 --> 00:08:58.950
could become a kind of
invisible totalitarianism

00:08:58.950 --> 00:09:02.310
founded on the domination of
technology-fueled consumerism.

00:09:02.310 --> 00:09:04.169
People would slowly lose their willpower

00:09:04.169 --> 00:09:07.225
and be controlled through the
manipulation of their desires.

00:09:07.225 --> 00:09:09.330
You know, like how TikTok does.

00:09:09.330 --> 00:09:10.350
Jurgen Habermas,

00:09:10.350 --> 00:09:12.930
one of the last living members
at the Frankfurt School,

00:09:12.930 --> 00:09:15.420
believed technology has its own logic

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:18.090
and threatens to destroy
not only politics,

00:09:18.090 --> 00:09:20.580
culture, and human life, but the world.

00:09:20.580 --> 00:09:23.250
Because, again, technology
can pull us away

00:09:23.250 --> 00:09:25.710
from both the essence of
our human subjectivity

00:09:25.710 --> 00:09:27.649
and our desire to find meaning

00:09:27.649 --> 00:09:29.880
beyond profit and productivity.

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:31.020
But as it turns out,

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:34.200
technology hasn't
destroyed everything yet,

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:35.996
except for my relationship
with my uncle Rob

00:09:35.996 --> 00:09:38.370
after he kept posting on Facebook.

00:09:38.370 --> 00:09:41.190
We can now communicate with
far-flowing friends and family

00:09:41.190 --> 00:09:43.980
more easily than ever
before, which is rad, again,

00:09:43.980 --> 00:09:45.570
except for with my uncle Rob.

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:47.700
we can have a lifesaving
bacon, egg, and cheese

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:49.980
sent to our homes with the tap of a button

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:51.750
As we ride in bed hungover.

00:09:51.750 --> 00:09:53.760
Hell, you can even get a pacemaker

00:09:53.760 --> 00:09:55.230
that tracks your cardiac health

00:09:55.230 --> 00:09:57.480
and sends potentially
life saving status updates

00:09:57.480 --> 00:09:58.313
to your doctor.

00:09:58.313 --> 00:10:01.650
But in spite of countless
net positive developments,

00:10:01.650 --> 00:10:05.190
new innovations like TikTok
can still freak us out.

00:10:05.190 --> 00:10:08.070
And the anxiety around
tech is alive and well.

00:10:08.070 --> 00:10:09.960
And some of this is valid.

00:10:09.960 --> 00:10:12.869
For one, social media and
other database innovations

00:10:12.869 --> 00:10:16.694
collect, circulate, share,
and profit off of our data

00:10:16.694 --> 00:10:18.330
on a global level.

00:10:18.330 --> 00:10:21.000
It's scary when our personal data is used

00:10:21.000 --> 00:10:22.920
for purposes outside of our control.

00:10:22.920 --> 00:10:25.200
It's double scary when TikTok's algorithm

00:10:25.200 --> 00:10:27.960
can so accurately predict
and control our behavior.

00:10:27.960 --> 00:10:31.080
If technology can make us
watch cat videos for hours,

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:34.560
what's to stop it from taking
over our lives completely?

00:10:34.560 --> 00:10:36.840
And what happens when
the videos it serves us

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:39.030
aren't just fun time wasters,

00:10:39.030 --> 00:10:41.400
but less fun conspiracy theories?

00:10:41.400 --> 00:10:43.770
Now, obviously, misinformation clogs up

00:10:43.770 --> 00:10:45.720
every social media platform

00:10:45.720 --> 00:10:47.880
as the Uncle robs of the Facebook world

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:50.970
unite to convince you that
there's now a microchip

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:53.220
in your happily vaccinated arm.

00:10:53.220 --> 00:10:55.980
but journalist and author,
Chris Stokel-Walker

00:10:55.980 --> 00:10:57.720
argues that TikTok's algorithm,

00:10:57.720 --> 00:10:59.790
coupled with the immediacy of its format,

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:02.580
makes it especially
right for misinformation.

00:11:02.580 --> 00:11:05.678
He writes, "Emotive videos
can cause people to overlook

00:11:05.678 --> 00:11:08.040
whether or not information is legitimate."

00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:10.110
This can have real consequences.

00:11:10.110 --> 00:11:11.889
Analysis for media from NewsGuard

00:11:11.889 --> 00:11:14.550
determined that misinformation about COVID

00:11:14.550 --> 00:11:17.790
reached a staggering
eight out of nine children

00:11:17.790 --> 00:11:20.190
after just 35 minutes on the app.

00:11:20.190 --> 00:11:22.560
Many adults are concerned in particular

00:11:22.560 --> 00:11:24.330
about potentially misleading videos

00:11:24.330 --> 00:11:26.610
on what's known as therapy tok.

00:11:26.610 --> 00:11:28.350
This is a trend in which Gen Z's

00:11:28.350 --> 00:11:29.959
self-diagnosis mental disorders,

00:11:29.959 --> 00:11:31.768
after watching influencer's videos

00:11:31.768 --> 00:11:33.630
chronicling their own struggles.

00:11:33.630 --> 00:11:36.810
Experts are split on
whether this is good or bad.

00:11:36.810 --> 00:11:37.710
On the one hand,

00:11:37.710 --> 00:11:40.560
influencers help normalize
discussions around mental health,

00:11:40.560 --> 00:11:43.408
but on the other, misdiagnosing
mental health issues

00:11:43.408 --> 00:11:45.630
can have serious consequences.

00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:47.730
To say nothing about the apps

00:11:47.730 --> 00:11:50.190
that are making it easier
than ever for young people

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:51.420
to get psych meds,

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:55.440
they may or may not need
sent directly to their homes.

00:11:55.440 --> 00:11:57.720
However, what's at the crux of this issue

00:11:57.720 --> 00:12:01.740
is our very human tendency
to trust popular people,

00:12:01.740 --> 00:12:05.100
even if they have no idea
what they're talking about.

00:12:05.100 --> 00:12:07.650
Historically, the most
popular people around

00:12:07.650 --> 00:12:08.910
have been celebrities,

00:12:08.910 --> 00:12:11.970
and the masses have loved
following their examples.

00:12:11.970 --> 00:12:14.910
Whether they're channeling
their inner Lord Byrons

00:12:14.910 --> 00:12:17.340
with roughly shirts,
raising AIDS awareness

00:12:17.340 --> 00:12:21.300
like Bono and Angelina Jolie,
or drinking high-end tequila

00:12:21.300 --> 00:12:23.070
because George Clooney
and his buddies make it.

00:12:23.070 --> 00:12:25.920
But celebrity influence can also backfire.

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:27.690
Remember Jenny McCarthy?

00:12:27.690 --> 00:12:32.220
Well, back in 2005, she used
her expertise as a parent

00:12:32.220 --> 00:12:34.230
to widely propagate the idea

00:12:34.230 --> 00:12:37.050
that the MMR vaccine
caused her son's autism.

00:12:37.050 --> 00:12:40.200
Even today, you might have
noticed that many Americans

00:12:40.200 --> 00:12:42.630
still aren't sure about vaccines.

00:12:42.630 --> 00:12:44.391
But whether you're turning to Tom Brady

00:12:44.391 --> 00:12:47.430
for nutritional advice, JLo
for her hair care routine,

00:12:47.430 --> 00:12:48.959
or buying a Subway foot long

00:12:48.959 --> 00:12:51.210
because Steph Curry told you to,

00:12:51.210 --> 00:12:52.912
at the end of the day, celebrities,

00:12:52.912 --> 00:12:56.520
they're just like us is
a punchline for a reason.

00:12:56.520 --> 00:12:58.140
They are not like us.

00:12:58.140 --> 00:13:01.350
So where does a feeling of
true trust and kinship exist

00:13:01.350 --> 00:13:02.820
in today's media landscape?

00:13:02.820 --> 00:13:06.570
TikTok, of course, where
folks who are just like you

00:13:06.570 --> 00:13:08.597
are able to have a
celebrity-like influence

00:13:08.597 --> 00:13:12.240
just by virtue of being themselves.

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:14.760
As you take in the sheer volume of content

00:13:14.760 --> 00:13:17.760
from your favorite creators,
it's easy to form what's called

00:13:17.760 --> 00:13:20.070
a parasocial relationship with them,

00:13:20.070 --> 00:13:22.110
i.e. one in which you feel like

00:13:22.110 --> 00:13:24.120
you already know that person in real life,

00:13:24.120 --> 00:13:27.120
even though they have no idea you exist.

00:13:27.120 --> 00:13:29.760
What's more, scholars Bhandari and Bimo

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:32.160
suggested in their analysis of TikTok

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:34.687
that in contrast to other
social media platforms,

00:13:34.687 --> 00:13:36.947
"The crux of interaction
is not between users

00:13:36.947 --> 00:13:41.130
and their social network, but
between a user in what we call

00:13:41.130 --> 00:13:43.380
an algorithmized version of self."

00:13:43.380 --> 00:13:44.760
So essentially,

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:47.640
TikTok compounds these
parasocial relationships

00:13:47.640 --> 00:13:50.040
with such algorithmic precision,

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:52.920
but not only are you looking
at your favorite creator,

00:13:52.920 --> 00:13:56.310
but it also feels like
you're looking into a mirror

00:13:56.310 --> 00:13:57.330
at yourself.

00:13:57.330 --> 00:13:58.717
You might be asking yourself,

00:13:58.717 --> 00:14:00.690
"Why are we, as a human race,

00:14:00.690 --> 00:14:03.690
predisposed to listening to
celebrities and influencers

00:14:03.690 --> 00:14:05.220
instead of qualified experts?"

00:14:05.220 --> 00:14:08.460
Well, there are evolutionary
and psychological reasons

00:14:08.460 --> 00:14:10.890
behind why we trust who we trust.

00:14:10.890 --> 00:14:12.840
As far as influencers are concerned,

00:14:12.840 --> 00:14:16.290
we often fall victim to prestige,
biased, social learning.

00:14:16.290 --> 00:14:18.349
In basic terms, this is
the tendency to trust

00:14:18.349 --> 00:14:21.120
and learn from people who
have gained attention,

00:14:21.120 --> 00:14:24.510
respect, and admiration for
success in a particular domain.

00:14:24.510 --> 00:14:27.480
In this case, TikTok
subscribers and views.

00:14:27.480 --> 00:14:29.520
This adaptive social learning strategy

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:32.136
developed as a shortcut to
identifying group members

00:14:32.136 --> 00:14:34.410
who are thriving without needing to assess

00:14:34.410 --> 00:14:37.110
a 16-year-old superstar's
individual merits.

00:14:37.110 --> 00:14:40.200
So is potentially harmful
and dangerous misinformation

00:14:40.200 --> 00:14:41.430
being spread on TikTok?

00:14:41.430 --> 00:14:42.300
Absolutely.

00:14:42.300 --> 00:14:45.060
But again, this isn't unique to TikTok.

00:14:45.060 --> 00:14:48.810
And Gen Z's reliance on therapy
tok for advice and guidance

00:14:48.810 --> 00:14:50.094
isn't what's standing in the way

00:14:50.094 --> 00:14:52.140
of them seeking mental healthcare.

00:14:52.140 --> 00:14:53.550
As many have pointed out,

00:14:53.550 --> 00:14:55.590
inaccessibility to mental healthcare

00:14:55.590 --> 00:14:58.242
is what drives young people
to therapy tok for answers

00:14:58.242 --> 00:14:59.700
in the first place.

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:03.180
So far, TikTok doesn't
seem particularly evil,

00:15:03.180 --> 00:15:05.550
but what about TikTok sides,

00:15:05.550 --> 00:15:08.910
AKA little niche communities
or echo chambers on the app?

00:15:08.910 --> 00:15:11.970
Could these result in greater
political polarization?

00:15:11.970 --> 00:15:12.840
Not really.

00:15:12.840 --> 00:15:14.790
Ironically, political polarization

00:15:14.790 --> 00:15:16.530
currently occurs most in generations

00:15:16.530 --> 00:15:19.680
that use social media
the least, AKA the olds.

00:15:19.680 --> 00:15:22.680
Plus, political polarization
has been growing in the US

00:15:22.680 --> 00:15:24.300
since the 1970s,

00:15:24.300 --> 00:15:27.180
long before Noodles
ever had a no bones day.

00:15:27.180 --> 00:15:29.522
But hey, it's a lot easier to blame TikTok

00:15:29.522 --> 00:15:32.550
than to address the real
problems behind polarization

00:15:32.550 --> 00:15:36.420
like growing income equality
lack of access to education,

00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:39.030
resistance to multiculturalism
and globalization,

00:15:39.030 --> 00:15:42.150
stagnant wages, and Taco
Bell continuing to tease us

00:15:42.150 --> 00:15:44.100
with the return of the Mexican pizza.

00:15:44.100 --> 00:15:47.550
So then TikTok could and
probably does cause polarization,

00:15:47.550 --> 00:15:49.980
but it's probably not the main culprit.

00:15:49.980 --> 00:15:53.010
In other words, human
behavior may be to blame

00:15:53.010 --> 00:15:54.162
and that makes us uncomfortable,

00:15:54.162 --> 00:15:58.980
so we use TikTok as a scapegoat
for serious societal issues.

00:15:58.980 --> 00:16:01.740
For example, there are lots
of studies floating around

00:16:01.740 --> 00:16:05.100
arguing that social media
increases anxiety, depression,

00:16:05.100 --> 00:16:06.150
and poor self-esteem.

00:16:06.150 --> 00:16:09.180
The problem is many of
those are correlational.

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:11.022
Researchers sort people into two groups;

00:16:11.022 --> 00:16:12.930
people who consume TikTok

00:16:12.930 --> 00:16:15.390
and people who suffer from
anxiety and depression.

00:16:15.390 --> 00:16:18.330
Then they check the Venn diagram
to see who's at the center.

00:16:18.330 --> 00:16:19.950
If it's enough people, eureka,

00:16:19.950 --> 00:16:23.130
TikTok must cause all
sorts of mental illnesses.

00:16:23.130 --> 00:16:24.480
Yeah, but not really.

00:16:24.480 --> 00:16:26.400
Correlational studies only tell us

00:16:26.400 --> 00:16:28.290
these two groups are interconnected.

00:16:28.290 --> 00:16:30.900
They don't tell us whether
TikTok or depression

00:16:30.900 --> 00:16:32.400
is the chicken or the egg,

00:16:32.400 --> 00:16:35.220
but longitudinal studies
on social media do.

00:16:35.220 --> 00:16:37.440
These aren't as common
because longitudinal studies

00:16:37.440 --> 00:16:39.060
take more time and money,

00:16:39.060 --> 00:16:41.340
and unless you're Santa
Claus, it's not exactly cheap

00:16:41.340 --> 00:16:43.170
to track thousands of kids over the years.

00:16:43.170 --> 00:16:45.510
However, researchers from
Brigham Young University,

00:16:45.510 --> 00:16:46.830
Finland and Canada,

00:16:46.830 --> 00:16:49.710
were able to secure the
funding for this very purpose

00:16:49.710 --> 00:16:51.350
and found no true correlation

00:16:51.350 --> 00:16:54.510
between social media and
anxiety and depression.

00:16:54.510 --> 00:16:56.422
Conversely, German and Canadian studies

00:16:56.422 --> 00:16:58.227
found depressed and or anxious people

00:16:58.227 --> 00:17:00.360
were more likely to use social media,

00:17:00.360 --> 00:17:02.550
and that depressive
symptoms could even predict

00:17:02.550 --> 00:17:03.661
social media usage.

00:17:03.661 --> 00:17:04.710
This could indicate

00:17:04.710 --> 00:17:06.550
that people dealing with mental illnesses

00:17:06.550 --> 00:17:10.290
might be drawn to the hyper
stimulation, dopamine rewards,

00:17:10.290 --> 00:17:12.780
and so on that social media facilitates.

00:17:12.780 --> 00:17:15.900
All of this strongly suggests
that our society's anxiety

00:17:15.900 --> 00:17:20.130
and depression is the chicken
and social media is the egg.

00:17:20.130 --> 00:17:20.963
So yes,

00:17:20.963 --> 00:17:24.300
TikTok is absolutely changing
the social media landscape,

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:26.820
but it's not fundamentally changing us.

00:17:26.820 --> 00:17:30.737
In a tale as old as time, we've
hyper-focused on technology

00:17:30.737 --> 00:17:33.480
and ignored the power
of our own psychology.

00:17:33.480 --> 00:17:35.936
To be clear, we're not
trying to say that TikTok

00:17:35.936 --> 00:17:37.740
is a societal good.

00:17:37.740 --> 00:17:39.690
As we discussed in our previous episode

00:17:39.690 --> 00:17:41.880
on Elon's potential
acquisition of Twitter,

00:17:41.880 --> 00:17:45.900
calling platforms good or bad
is kind of missing the point.

00:17:45.900 --> 00:17:48.686
TikTok is used by influencers
and wanna-be influencers.

00:17:48.686 --> 00:17:51.270
It's used by nurses,
teachers, and therapists.

00:17:51.270 --> 00:17:54.180
It's also used by, say,
radical extremists.

00:17:54.180 --> 00:17:56.520
However, the knee jerk
reaction to judge it

00:17:56.520 --> 00:18:00.000
as inherently evil or
dangerous is misguided.

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:04.080
A more apt description might
be TikTok is chaotic neutral.

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:06.690
The reality is that
TikTok is a free product

00:18:06.690 --> 00:18:09.270
whose chief source of
income is ad revenue.

00:18:09.270 --> 00:18:10.876
And the only way to increase that income

00:18:10.876 --> 00:18:14.460
is to keep people on the
app for as long as possible.

00:18:14.460 --> 00:18:16.620
Our attention is a valuable resource

00:18:16.620 --> 00:18:19.590
and boy, oh boy, is it in demand.

00:18:19.590 --> 00:18:21.390
In this new attention economy,

00:18:21.390 --> 00:18:24.510
focus is our scarcest and
most valuable resource.

00:18:24.510 --> 00:18:27.360
So how do we keep social
media companies like TikTok

00:18:27.360 --> 00:18:29.730
from stealing it right
out of our time wallets?

00:18:29.730 --> 00:18:32.490
Author Mark Manson
proposes an attention diet

00:18:32.490 --> 00:18:35.430
where we identify worthwhile
information in relationships,

00:18:35.430 --> 00:18:37.470
cut out the junk, and cultivate habits

00:18:37.470 --> 00:18:40.380
that foster deeper focus
and longer attention spans.

00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:42.905
The goal is to focus on
what adds value to your life

00:18:42.905 --> 00:18:44.970
by being more intentional

00:18:44.970 --> 00:18:47.250
about what we consume on social media

00:18:47.250 --> 00:18:48.630
and how long we spend there.

00:18:48.630 --> 00:18:51.390
This is obviously easier said than done.

00:18:51.390 --> 00:18:54.819
But maybe, just maybe, if we
can build our mental fortitude

00:18:54.819 --> 00:18:57.900
like we build our imaginary
investment portfolios,

00:18:57.900 --> 00:19:01.410
we might just be able to
beat the algorithm after all.

00:19:01.410 --> 00:19:02.400
But what do you guys think?

00:19:02.400 --> 00:19:03.900
Can we win our attention back

00:19:03.900 --> 00:19:05.820
from the world's most effective algorithm?

00:19:05.820 --> 00:19:07.380
Do we even want our attention back

00:19:07.380 --> 00:19:08.790
if it means being bored as?

00:19:08.790 --> 00:19:10.830
Let us know what you
think in the comments.

